@startuml
!include _style.puml
!pragma layout smetana

title Trust Zones: Mesh Network Reachability

skinparam nodesep 30
skinparam ranksep 50
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11

' userLimited: desaturated ice-blue/grey — unauthenticated, restricted reach.
skinparam node<<userLimited>> {
  BackgroundColor #C9D6DE/#A8BAC7
  BorderColor #5C6E7A
  FontColor #14202B
}
' userFull: same ice-blue family as <<actor>>, but deeper/more saturated —
' authenticated, full reach.
skinparam node<<userFull>> {
  BackgroundColor #9CC3E0/#6FA0C9
  BorderColor #2C5C8F
  FontColor #0A1B2E
}
' <<service>> intentionally NOT redefined here: inherited as-is from
' _style.puml (navy blue family). Do not add a local block for it — see
' _style.puml comment on skinparam merging per-selector, not per-block.

' <<certAuthority>> (component/node) is centralized in _style.puml: the
' Certificate-Authority node uses that shared identity everywhere it
' appears, not a locally redefined color here.

' meshCoordinator: Headscale's own dedicated identity, matching
' 01-architecture-container.puml / 02-architecture-deployment.puml exactly
' (not centralized in _style.puml, so redeclared here per-file, all three
' properties together per the local-override gotcha).
skinparam node<<meshCoordinator>> {
  BackgroundColor #4A7E9E/#345A73
  BorderColor #1F3E4E
  FontColor #F0F0F0
}

package "Unauthenticated User Zone" {
  ' Trailing blank padding on the last label line is a deliberate spacer
  ' (same idiom as the blank quoted edge labels below, applied to a node
  ' label instead): it widens UnauthUser just enough to pull this
  ' package's left border into line with Authenticated User Zone's left
  ' border. AuthUser (below) carries an analogous, smaller trailing-space
  ' pad on its own last line: without it, UnauthUser filled ~92% of its
  ' zone's width while AuthUser filled only ~59% of its own, an
  ' inconsistent look the user flagged. Padding AuthUser too (verified via
  ' a joint grid search over both nodes' pad lengths, since growing either
  ' node measurably shifts UnauthUser's zone's left border) reaches ~93%
  ' vs ~88% fill, a close and visually consistent match, while ALSO
  ' landing both zones' left borders pixel-identical (0px gap, better than
  ' the previous 1px). Costs a small, verified increase in the
  ' zone-to-mesh gap (~2.0cm -> ~2.6cm, unchanged from prior rounds, not a
  ' new cost of this fix); every other invariant (row alignment, node
  ' centering, Internal Mesh internal grid) was re-verified unaffected.
  ' Attempted swapping this package's declaration order with Authenticated
  ' User Zone below (user wanted Authenticated on top) - even though Mesh
  ' VPN Zone's own declaration position didn't change, swapping just the
  ' two satellite packages' relative order was enough to flip the whole
  ' canvas under smetana (Mesh VPN Zone moved to canvas-left, its internal
  ' columns reversed, both User Zones moved to canvas-right). Reverted;
  ' order swap is not safe here, at least not via declaration order alone.
  node "**User**\nregistered,\nnot logged in                         " as UnauthUser <<userLimited>>
}

package "Authenticated User Zone" {
  ' See the fill-consistency note above: this trailing pad is tuned
  ' jointly with UnauthUser's own pad, not independently.
  node "**User**\nregistered\n& logged in              " as AuthUser <<userFull>>
}

package "Mesh VPN Zone" {
  ' Entry-Hub and Security-Switch (row 1) each get a leading + trailing
  ' blank label line: a deliberate spacer that grows their box height to
  ' match UnauthUser's (3-line label) height exactly, symmetric around
  ' their already-correct row center. This pulls Internal Mesh's own top
  ' border up to match Unauthenticated User Zone's top border pixel-for-
  ' pixel (55.2969 -> 41.2969) without moving the row-1 center alignment
  ' that was already correct (verified via rendered SVG coordinates).
  node "\n**Entry-Hub**\n" as EntryHub <<service>>
  node "\n**Security-Switch**\n" as SecuritySwitch <<service>>
  node "**Storage-Service**" as StorageService <<service>>
  node "**Network-Manager**" as NetworkManager <<service>>
  node "**Database-Vault**" as DatabaseVault <<service>>
  node "**Certificate-Authority**" as CA <<certAuthority>>

  ' Column 1 top-to-bottom: Entry-Hub, Storage-Service, Database-Vault.
  ' Doubled: a single hidden edge left Entry-Hub/Storage-Service ~10px off
  ' center under the pull of the diagonal Security-Switch<-->Database-Vault
  ' edge below; the second hidden edge adds pull strength to hold the
  ' column centered (verified via rendered SVG coordinates).
  EntryHub -[hidden]down- StorageService
  EntryHub -[hidden]down- StorageService
  ' Column 2 top-to-bottom: Security-Switch, Network-Manager,
  ' Certificate-Authority (CA has no explicit placement instruction this
  ' round; it trails Network-Manager, the component it is most closely
  ' related to). Doubled for the same reason as column 1 above: the
  ' diagonal edge to Database-Vault pulls Security-Switch off-center
  ' relative to Network-Manager unless countered.
  SecuritySwitch -[hidden]down- NetworkManager
  SecuritySwitch -[hidden]down- NetworkManager
  ' Tripled (was doubled): the new MeshBaseWaypoint edges below pull both
  ' DatabaseVault and CA toward one shared target, which was enough to flip
  ' their column order (verified via rendered SVG x-coordinates - CA ended
  ' up left, DatabaseVault right, opposite of every prior round this
  ' session). Extra hidden-edge weight here plus the new explicit
  ' StorageService-DatabaseVault pull below re-anchors column 1 (Entry-Hub/
  ' Storage-Service/Database-Vault) vs column 2 (Security-Switch/Network-
  ' Manager/CA) strongly enough to hold against that new competing pull.
  NetworkManager -[hidden]down- CA
  NetworkManager -[hidden]down- CA
  ' New: Database-Vault previously had no hidden-edge column anchor of its
  ' own, only the real StorageService<-->DatabaseVault edge - not enough
  ' once MeshBaseWaypoint introduced competing pull on both bottom-row
  ' nodes. This explicitly reinforces column 1.
  StorageService -[hidden]down- DatabaseVault
  StorageService -[hidden]down- DatabaseVault
}

' Headscale is deliberately its own package, declared after Internal Mesh
' (smetana places same-rank satellite packages by declaration order: after
' the big cluster lands it on canvas-right, mirroring the User Zones on
' canvas-left). It is NOT nested inside Internal Mesh: unlike every other
' component there, Headscale is reachable over the public network, not by
' mesh placement (NM-F-12) - this package exists to make that exception
' visible rather than misrepresenting Headscale as mesh-reachable.
package "Public Zone" {
  node "**Headscale**" as Headscale <<meshCoordinator>>
}

' Unauthenticated Zone stacks above Authenticated Zone (reverted swap).
UnauthUser -[hidden]down- AuthUser

' Unauthenticated Zone row-aligned with Entry-Hub (row 1). The blank
' quoted label is a deliberate spacer: global nodesep/ranksep bumps and
' PlantUML's dash-length rank-distance trick both have zero effect on the
' user-zone-to-Internal-Mesh gap under smetana (verified); a wide blank
' edge label is the lever that actually reserves extra horizontal room
' here, and as a side effect also recenters the User node inside its
' zone package (previously hugging the package's right edge). The
' relative width between this label and the one below controls both
' zones' centering jointly, not just their own zone; tune both together.
UnauthUser -right-> EntryHub : "                  "
' Authenticated Zone row-aligned with Storage-Service (row 2); the
' relationship to Entry-Hub still targets row 1, so it renders as an
' upward/diagonal arrow. See the spacer-label note above: this label
' must stay 8 spaces wider than Unauthenticated Zone's above to keep
' both User nodes centered in their packages (verified via rendered SVG
' coordinates; the two labels' relative width, not the absolute size,
' is what balances the centering).
AuthUser -[hidden]right- StorageService
AuthUser -up-> EntryHub
AuthUser -right-> StorageService : "             "

' Row 1, column 1 -> column 2.
EntryHub -right-> SecuritySwitch
' Column 1, row 1 -> row 2 (DV-F-09 / ST-F-11, bidirectional per KI-07).
StorageService <-down-> DatabaseVault
' Security-Switch (row 1, column 2) to Database-Vault (row 3, column 1):
' not a clean row/column pairing, renders as a longer diagonal hop.
SecuritySwitch <--> DatabaseVault
' Column 2, row 1 -> row 2.
SecuritySwitch <-down-> NetworkManager

' Network-Manager to Headscale (NM-F-12): the one inter-service call in
' this diagram NOT gated by network placement, only by mTLS + token. The
' direction hint also does the rank-ordering job of a hidden edge (no
' redundant hidden edge needed alongside a real one between the same pair).
NetworkManager -right-> Headscale : "           "

legend
  Certificate-Authority can reach, and be reached by,
  every internal component (NM-F-04).
end legend

@enduml
